\alias{BaseRSpineplotLayerProcessor}
\title{Base R Spine Plot Layer Processor}
\description{
Reads \code{spineplot()} as the two-way contingency table it draws.

A spine plot is a mosaic of one categorical axis against another: one
column per level of \code{x}, its \strong{width} that level's share of all
observations, split vertically by \code{y}'s conditional proportions inside it.
That is the shape \code{BaseRMosaicLayerProcessor} was written for in #247 --
"the column widths encode data too" -- so a spine plot is read as a
\code{mosaic} layer, and this class changes only the two things that differ.

\strong{Where the table comes from.} \code{mosaicplot()} is handed its table, so the
recorded call carries it. \code{spineplot()} is handed the two variables and
builds the table itself, and it has \strong{no \code{plot} argument} to be asked for
the table without drawing -- \code{cdplot()} has one, \code{spineplot()} does not.
But it \emph{returns} what it drew:

\preformatted{
spineplot(table(f, g))
#       g
# f      no yes
#   low  11  12
#   mid   7  14
#   high 12   4
}

so the call is replayed on a throwaway device and the return value taken.
\code{graphics::spineplot} by the qualified name, not the bare one: maidr
patches the name on the search path, and the bare call would record the
replay as a second chart. Reproducing the binning by hand instead would be
a re-derivation -- \code{spineplot} cuts a numeric \code{x} by its own rule -- and
the point of every reading in this package is that the library is asked
rather than imitated.

\strong{Where the tiles are.} \code{mosaicplot()} writes one \code{polygon} grob per
cell. \code{spineplot()} writes \strong{one \code{rect} grob for the whole panel}, and
gridSVG exports it as one \verb{<rect>} element per tile. Measured on a 3x2
table:

\preformatted{
graphics-plot-1-rect-1.1.1   x  59.04   w 152.86   h 118.71
graphics-plot-1-rect-1.1.2   x  59.04   w 152.86   h 108.81
graphics-plot-1-rect-1.1.3   x 219.88   w 139.57   h 151.68
graphics-plot-1-rect-1.1.4   x 219.88   w 139.57   h  75.84
graphics-plot-1-rect-1.1.5   x 367.42   w 106.34   h  56.88
graphics-plot-1-rect-1.1.6   x 367.42   w 106.34   h 170.64
}

Six elements for six cells, sharing an x within a column, and the widths
152.86 : 139.57 : 106.34 in the marginals' own ratio 23 : 21 : 16. The
order is column-major, and \strong{within a column the last fill level is drawn
first}: the heights pair 118.71 : 108.81 with 12 : 11, which is \code{yes}
above \code{no}. That is the opposite of the mosaic's ascending order, which is
why the index is computed here rather than inherited.

\strong{A zero cell still draws.} The hazard this shape invites is the one
xability/maidr#1002 found elsewhere: a count of zero skipped rather than
drawn, shifting every later tile's index by one. Measured on a table with
a genuine zero in it, \code{spineplot} draws the tile anyway with \code{height="0"},
so the positional pairing holds. There is a test for exactly that.
}
