\subsection{\code{BaseRSpineplotLayerProcessor$generate_selectors()}}{
  Address the tile each cell was drawn into

One \code{rect} grob holds the whole panel, so the tiles are its
sub-elements rather than grobs of their own and are addressed by
their exported ids. The grob is \emph{found} rather than named by
formula, and a panel that does not hold exactly one is declined: a
guessed id resolves to nothing at best and to another panel's tiles
at worst, and a spine plot with no highlight still reads (#145).

The index runs column-major with the fill levels \strong{descending}
inside a column, which is the order measured off the drawing and
recorded in the class note. The emitted list is in the payload's own
order -- all categories of the first fill, then the second -- so the
two line up.
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{BaseRSpineplotLayerProcessor$generate_selectors(layer_info, gt = NULL)}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Arguments}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="arguments">}}
    \describe{
      \item{\code{layer_info}}{Information about the recorded plot call}
      \item{\code{gt}}{Gtable object to search}
    }
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Returns}{
    List of selectors, one per cell, or empty when unresolvable
  }
}

